#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106643} :activity/id #uuid "e068c66e-c6f9-5b98-88d1-321d4bd52ecb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050650 :topic/id #uuid "5c5917ee-8504-5c4f-8c53-d54f4a7e936f" :topic/math-academy-id 1321} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106643 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107162 :tutorial/content "Consider the polygon $ABCD$, where the side $\\overline{AB}$ is extended beyond the vertex $B.$\n\n![](images/fa/fa23c036920e2c506c38f1e7be8a498c0a349ef145e8c444201665345bc648e4.png)\n\nWe call $\\color{blue}\\angle{CBE}$ the **exterior angle** of the vertex $B$.\n\nGiven a polygon's vertex, the interior and exterior angles are supplementary. So, for the vertex $B,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\color{YellowOrange}m\\angle{ABC}}_{\\large\\text{Interior}} \\:+\\: \\underbrace{\\color{blue}m\\angle{CBE}}_{\\large\\text{Exterior}} = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nWe have the following theorem:\n\nThe measures of the exterior angles of any simple polygon sum to $360^\\circ.$\n\nWe'll show why this theorem is true at the end of the lesson.\n\nLet's label the exterior angles of our polygon below:\n\n![](images/ac/acc0ac90f27a78e83b6b14651f61ddfe6a87afa56b6e51a1af57f5e74c31689f.png)\n\nAccording to our theorem, these four angles sum to $360^\\circ.$\n\n$$\na+b+c+d = 360^\\circ\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6cb4a5ba-6c28-59e2-ab72-89e4f5f69ae3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8041 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4bf926eb-9821-545f-b67e-d2d50c4f61f0" :step/math-academy-id 187128 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106644}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106644 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105749 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105471 :question/id #uuid "0cd03b60-baf3-5689-9f84-5f8be9853a9b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12838"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9846ca00-8305-56a8-a14d-7aa2488c0016" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048201}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506538}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Exterior Angles"} :step/id #uuid "2d241585-6a66-55ed-8dd4-a690566db2a8" :step/math-academy-id 187129 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106645}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106645 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105750 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105472 :question/id #uuid "fbc903e6-45ee-53c5-9d31-0edf8140cacd" :question/math-academy-id "e-12839"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5de9bf2f-b11c-5af0-a569-5f25aebae227" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048725}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506512}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Exterior Angles"} :step/id #uuid "80b138e7-13bc-505c-9c7b-e57531dd484d" :step/math-academy-id 187132 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106646}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106646 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105751 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105473 :question/id #uuid "44ea9915-91ca-5abf-a1ac-bb801069ef24" :question/math-academy-id "e-12879"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0794b384-c2ff-5d4a-b325-28077b5b1f96" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050452}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506557}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining an Unknown Quantity Using Exterior Angles"} :step/id #uuid "c27f1faa-2fca-5dc4-8283-8cf462884593" :step/math-academy-id 187135 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106647}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106647 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105752 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105474 :question/id #uuid "e96a16e9-122f-582c-beb9-22ca0d4d7d68" :question/math-academy-id "e-12880"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64712488-234f-5d44-92a9-6a4821f2d304" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050127}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106488} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506562}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Exterior Angle by Determining an Unknown Quantity"} :step/id #uuid "f65fca30-a9ac-5101-b87a-1c01743c0b0b" :step/math-academy-id 187138 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106648}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106648 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107163 :tutorial/content "Why do the exterior angles of a polygon always sum to $360^\\circ?$\n\nSuppose we have an $n$-sided polygon. Then, we have the following:\n\n- There are $n$ pairs of interior-exterior angles, each pair being supplementary (i.e., their measures add to $180^\\circ).$\n\n- Therefore, the sum of the measures of *all* interior-exterior pairs equals ${\\color{blue}n\\cdot 180^\\circ}.$\n\n- In a previous lesson, we saw that the sum of the *interior* angles equals ${\\color{red}{(n-2)\\cdot 180^\\circ}}.$\n\nTherefore, to get the sum of the *exterior* angles, we subtract ${\\color{red}{(n-2)\\cdot 180^\\circ}}$ from ${\\color{blue}n\\cdot 180^\\circ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Sum of Exterior Angles} & =n\\cdot {180}^{∘}-(n-2)\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\  & =n\\cdot {180}^{∘}-n\\cdot {180}^{∘}+2\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\cancel{n\\cdot {180}^{∘}}-\\cancel{n\\cdot {180}^{∘}}+2\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis proves that the sum of the exterior angles of a (simple) polygon equals $360^\\circ.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fb5a1532-47ec-5455-96c8-49187460e543" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8042 :tutorial/title "Proving the Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "ee786271-6101-5bf4-9d57-2fe763770d7a" :step/math-academy-id 187141}] :activity/title "Exterior Angles of Polygons" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105975}]}
